{
  "decision": "PROMOTE",
  "why": "Zero errors and latency that converges to stable parity by the end of the test. Evidence: (1) Error budget is clean - 0 5xx / 0.00% err on canary across all 2476 requests, on every route (/browse 1181, /search 781, /checkout 514), and in all four 30s windows. (2) The over-time breakdown shows the canary's latency penalty is a transient warm-up, not a regression: canary p95 goes 210 -> 130 -> 95 -> 94 ms while stable stays flat at 93/94/95/94 ms. For the last 60s (half the test) the canary is indistinguishable from stable (95 vs 95, then 94 vs 94). (3) The scary aggregate numbers (canary p95 177 vs stable 94, p50 62 vs 34, /checkout p95 215 vs 103) are an averaging artifact: the canary served ~2476 requests over 120s, so the slow 0-30s window is ~25% of its sample and drags every aggregate and per-route figure up. The per-route tables are not broken out over time, so they inherit the same contamination and should not be read as steady-state. Judgement: the decay pattern of a single fresh pod (cold caches, JIT/warm-up, connection pool filling) matches 210 -> 94 exactly; a real performance regression in 1.1 would hold its elevated p95 for the whole 120s instead of landing on stable's number and staying there. Risk read: promoting costs a brief warm-up latency bump on the newly rolled pods (no errors, well under typical timeouts), whereas rolling back blocks the release on a signal that has already resolved itself. Recommended guardrail, since steady state is only ~60s of data: promote with a surge/readiness-gated rolling update so pods warm before taking full traffic, and watch p95 and 5xx for the first 10 minutes at 100%, with /checkout (p95 103 ms stable) as the route to abort on."
}

{
  "decision": "PROMOTE",
  "why": "No error signal anywhere: 0 5xx and 0.00% err% on both arms (stable 7343 reqs, canary 2476 reqs) and on every route (/browse, /search, /checkout). The only adverse signal is latency, and the over-time breakdown shows it is warmup, not a steady-state regression: canary p95 falls 210 -> 130 -> 95 -> 94 ms across the four 30 s windows while stable p95 stays flat at 93/94/95/94 ms, so the canary reaches parity by 60 s and holds it for the final 60 s. The whole-test aggregates that look bad (canary p95 177 vs 94, p50 62 vs 34, /checkout p95 215 vs 103) are averages dominated by that first 30-60 s of cold start, so they overstate the steady-state cost. Load is also comparably distributed per pod: the single canary pod served 2476 requests vs roughly 2448 per stable pod, so the canary is not flattered by being under-loaded. Judgement: the steady-state evidence supports promotion. Caveats (judgement, not measured): the sample is one pod and ~2476 requests with zero errors, which cannot rule out a rare fault, and the warmup penalty will recur on each newly started pod. Recommend a staged rollout with readiness/startup probes and surge control rather than replacing all three stable pods at once, and watch 5xx and p95 for the first few minutes after each step."
}
